export const MR1={title:'The event and the Medical Reserve Corps',hook:'When a hurricane fills shelters, a measles outbreak calls for vaccination clinics or a heat wave threatens older adults, local health departments need extra hands—fast. Medical Reserve Corps volunteers fill that gap in communities across the country. MRC Partnership challenges your HOSA chapter to work side by side with a local MRC unit for a full year and show the difference you made.',objectives:['Describe the MRC Partnership event, its rounds and how it is scored.','Explain the history, mission and structure of the Medical Reserve Corps.','Describe who MRC volunteers are and what they do.','Explain why HOSA and the MRC are natural partners.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'MRC Partnership is a **team event for 2–6 members**. Every chapter activity for this event **must be done in partnership with a Medical Reserve Corps unit**—there are **no exceptions**, and a partnership with the MRC **outside the classroom** must be in place for activities to count. Activities must take place between **July 1, 2026 and May 15, 2027**. All **social media posts** about the event must be **approved by the MRC partner** before posting, or the team may lose the opportunity to compete.',
'**Round One** is a pre-judged **portfolio** of up to **16 numbered pages**, uploaded by **one team member** as **one PDF** by **May 15**; it is worth **60 points**. **Round Two** is a **five-minute presentation** worth **70 points**—**50** for content and **20** for delivery. Notecards are allowed, but **no other materials, props or visual aids**. All team members must take an active role.',
'The guidelines stress **quality over quantity**. Judges want to see how your chapter worked with the MRC to identify and address a **specific community need**, and the **impact** of that work—not a long list of small, disconnected activities.',
{t:'table',caption:'How the event is scored',head:['Part','What is judged','Points'],rows:[['Round One portfolio','Title page, verification form, logistics document, partnership description, activities, neatness, formatting, page limit','60'],['Round Two content','Cooperative work, MRC mission, community impact, chapter impact, creativity','50'],['Round Two delivery','Voice, stage presence, diction, team participation','20'],['Total','','130']]}]],
['What the Medical Reserve Corps is',[
'The **Medical Reserve Corps (MRC)** is a national network of **community-based volunteer units** that strengthen public health, emergency response and community resilience. It was created in **2002**, after the September 11, 2001 attacks showed that many medical and public health professionals wanted to help but had no organized way to do so. Today the national program is housed in the **Administration for Strategic Preparedness and Response (ASPR)** within the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services.',
'Each **MRC unit** is organized locally—often within a health department, emergency management agency, hospital, university or nonprofit—and is led by a **unit leader** (coordinator). Units recruit, train and organize volunteers to meet **local** needs. **State coordinators** and **regional liaisons** support units and can help HOSA chapters find a partner.',
'Because units are local, no two are exactly alike. A rural unit might focus on flu clinics and wildfire smoke, while an urban unit might staff cooling centers and support large public events. Learning your unit’s priorities is the first step to a strong partnership.',
match('MRC term and meaning',[['MRC unit','A local, community-based volunteer group'],['Unit leader','Coordinates the local unit and its volunteers'],['State coordinator','Supports MRC units across a state'],['Regional liaison','Helps chapters and units connect'],['ASPR','Federal office that houses the national MRC program'],['Founded','2002, after September 11']])]],
['MRC volunteers and their work',[
'MRC volunteers include **medical professionals** (physicians, nurses, pharmacists, EMTs, veterinarians), **public health professionals** and **community members without medical training** who help with logistics, communication, registration and translation. Many units welcome youth through partnerships like this one, though age requirements and roles vary by unit.',
'In **emergencies**, MRC volunteers staff **shelters**, **points of dispensing (PODs)** for vaccines or medications, **call centers** and medical stations. In **non-emergency** times, they support **vaccination clinics**, health screenings, preparedness education, **CPR** and **Stop the Bleed** training, community health fairs and large public events. Volunteers train regularly so they are ready when needed.',
'This dual role—**everyday public health** plus **emergency readiness**—is why MRC units are valuable to their communities. Volunteers who already know their community and have trained together can respond quickly and safely.',
sort('Emergency or non-emergency MRC activity?',['Emergency response','Non-emergency activity'],[['Staffing a hurricane shelter',0],['Running a back-to-school vaccination clinic',1],['Dispensing medication after an anthrax threat',0],['Teaching CPR at a community center',1],['Answering a call line during a disease outbreak',0],['First aid station at a marathon',1]])]],
['The MRC mission',[
'The MRC’s mission centers on **improving the health and safety of communities** by **organizing and using public health, medical and other volunteers**. Its work supports three broad goals that appear throughout this event: **strengthening public health**, **increasing emergency response capabilities** and **building community resilience**.',
'In Round Two, judges score your **understanding of the MRC mission**, and the highest score requires **four or more examples** of the mission in action. Each activity in your portfolio should connect clearly to the mission—for example, a preparedness fair strengthens resilience; a mock disaster drill builds response capability; helping at a flu clinic strengthens public health.',
'Practice explaining the mission in your own words and linking it to each of your activities. A clear, confident connection between what you did and why the MRC exists is one of the most persuasive parts of a presentation.',
chk('How many examples of the MRC mission earn the highest Round Two score for that criterion?',['Four or more','One','Two','None are needed'],'Strong evidence requires four or more examples.')]],
['Why HOSA and the MRC partner',[
'HOSA members are **future health professionals**; the MRC needs **trained, motivated volunteers** and wants to build the next generation of public health and emergency responders. A partnership gives students real-world experience in **public health**, **emergency preparedness**, **teamwork** and **community service**, while units gain energetic helpers, youth perspectives and connections to schools and families.',
'Partnerships can also introduce students to **careers**—nursing, emergency medicine, epidemiology, emergency management, pharmacy and public health—through mentoring and shadowing. Judges score **impact on the HOSA chapter** as well as impact on the community, so track how the partnership changed your members’ skills, knowledge and interests.',
'The best partnerships benefit **both** sides. Ask your unit leader what would be most helpful to them and look for ways your chapter’s strengths—social media skills, school connections, language abilities—can meet those needs.',
sort('Benefit to the MRC unit or to the HOSA chapter?',['MRC unit','HOSA chapter'],[['Extra volunteers for a vaccination clinic',0],['Students learn emergency preparedness skills',1],['Outreach to schools and families',0],['Mentoring from health professionals',1],['Youth perspective on preparedness messages',0],['Exposure to public health careers',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Starting the year',{a:{text:'It is August. Your chapter wants to compete in MRC Partnership. (Scenario invented for practice.) What must be true for activities to count?',choices:[['They must be done in partnership with an MRC unit between July 1 and May 15','b','Correct.',true],['Any volunteering counts, with or without the MRC','b','There are no exceptions.',false]]},b:{text:'A teammate wants to post photos of your first event right away. What must happen first?',choices:[['The MRC partner must approve the post','c','Correct.',true],['Nothing—post immediately','c','Unapproved posts risk disqualification.',false]]},c:{text:'You plan fewer, higher-quality activities tied to a clear community need.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Team of 2–6; all activities must be done with an MRC unit between July 1, 2026 and May 15, 2027; MRC must approve social media posts; portfolio (60 points, ≤16 pages, one PDF by May 15) plus a 5-minute presentation (70 points) with only notecards; quality over quantity.','The MRC (created in 2002, housed in ASPR within HHS) is a network of local volunteer units led by unit leaders and supported by state coordinators and regional liaisons; volunteers include medical, public health and nonmedical members.','The mission is improving community health and safety by organizing volunteers—strengthening public health, emergency response capability and resilience; judges want four or more mission examples and impact on both the community and the HOSA chapter.'],terms:[['Medical Reserve Corps','A national network of local volunteer units supporting public health and emergency response.'],['Unit leader','The coordinator of a local MRC unit.'],['Point of dispensing (POD)','A site that gives out vaccines or medications to many people during an emergency.']],mcqs:[q('When must MRC Partnership activities take place?','July 1, 2026–May 15, 2027','January–December 2026','Any time','Only in summer','Competition year.'),q('Who must approve social media posts about the event?','The MRC partner','No one','Only the team captain','The school newspaper','Required.'),q('What is the maximum length of the portfolio?','16 numbered pages','50 pages','5 pages','No limit','Pages above 16 are not judged.'),q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','2 minutes','20 minutes','Notecards only.'),q('Which federal office houses the national MRC program?','ASPR','FEMA only','CDC only','USDA','Within HHS.'),q('When was the MRC created?','2002','1950','1990','2020','After September 11.'),q('Which may volunteer in the MRC?','Medical, public health and nonmedical community members','Only physicians','Only nurses','Only firefighters','Many skills.'),q('What do judges value most in activities?','Quality and impact over quantity','The largest number of activities','The most photos','The longest descriptions','Address a specific need.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain what the Medical Reserve Corps is, describe its mission and explain why a HOSA–MRC partnership benefits both the community and the HOSA chapter.',rubric:[['MRC basics',['2002','september 11','aspr','hhs','local','units','volunteers','unit leader']],['Mission',['health','safety','communities','volunteers','public health','emergency response','resilience']],['Activities',['shelter','vaccination','pod','training','cpr','events','preparedness']],['Mutual benefit',['hosa','future health professionals','experience','careers','mentoring','volunteers','chapter impact']]],model:'The Medical Reserve Corps is a national network of community-based volunteer units that strengthen public health, emergency response and community resilience. It was created in 2002 after the September 11 attacks showed that many health professionals wanted to help but lacked an organized way to do so, and today the national program is housed in the Administration for Strategic Preparedness and Response within HHS. Each local unit is led by a unit leader and supported by state coordinators and regional liaisons, and its volunteers include physicians, nurses, pharmacists and public health workers as well as community members without medical training. The MRC’s mission is to improve the health and safety of communities by organizing and using public health, medical and other volunteers. In emergencies, volunteers staff shelters, points of dispensing and call centers; in everyday life, they support vaccination clinics, health fairs, CPR and Stop the Bleed training and large public events. A HOSA–MRC partnership benefits the community because students add energy, outreach to schools and families and youth-friendly preparedness messages to the unit’s work. It benefits the HOSA chapter because members gain real experience in public health and emergency preparedness, teamwork and leadership skills, mentoring from professionals and exposure to careers such as nursing, epidemiology and emergency management.'},sources:[RULES,MRC,MRC_ABOUT]};

export const MR2={title:'Preparedness, health security and community resilience',hook:'Disasters do not affect everyone equally. An older adult who uses oxygen, a family without a car and a child with asthma all face extra risks when the power goes out or a storm floods the roads. Preparedness and resilience are about planning ahead so the whole community can withstand, respond to and recover from emergencies. These ideas, drawn from the event’s suggested resources, should shape your activities.',objectives:['Describe the phases of emergency management.','Summarize the goals of the National Health Security Strategy and disaster risk reduction.','Explain community resilience and why vulnerable populations need special attention.','Describe ways young people can contribute to preparedness.'],sections:[
['The emergency management cycle',[
'Emergency management is often described as a cycle of four phases. **Mitigation** reduces the likelihood or impact of disasters (building codes, flood barriers, vaccination). **Preparedness** builds the ability to respond (plans, kits, training, drills, volunteer recruitment). **Response** saves lives and meets immediate needs during and right after an emergency (rescue, shelters, medical care). **Recovery** restores the community over weeks to years (rebuilding, mental health support, economic recovery).',
'MRC units contribute to **every phase**: educating the public (preparedness), training volunteers (preparedness), staffing shelters and PODs (response) and supporting health services and emotional recovery afterward (recovery). Your chapter’s activities can also be mapped to these phases, which helps judges see their purpose.',
'Many experts now add **prevention** and **protection** to the cycle, especially for threats like disease outbreaks and terrorism. Whatever model you use, the key idea is that preparedness is continuous—communities learn from each event and get ready for the next.',
sort('Which phase?',['Mitigation','Preparedness','Response','Recovery'],[['Raising homes above flood level',0],['Practicing a mass vaccination drill',1],['Running a shelter during a hurricane',2],['Counseling services months after a tornado',3],['Distributing 72-hour kit supply lists',1],['Setting up a first aid station after an earthquake',2]])]],
['Health security and the National Health Security Strategy',[
'**Health security** means that a nation and its communities are prepared for, protected from and resilient to health threats—natural disasters, disease outbreaks, chemical or radiological incidents and other emergencies. The **National Health Security Strategy (NHSS)**, prepared by ASPR, sets national priorities for building and sustaining health security.',
'The strategy emphasizes **community resilience**, **strong public health and health care systems**, **effective medical countermeasures** (vaccines, medications, supplies), **coordination** among government, private and volunteer partners and **equity**, so that preparedness reaches everyone. MRC units are a key part of the volunteer side of this system.',
'For your portfolio, connect activities to these priorities: a CPR class builds community capability; a vaccination clinic supports public health and countermeasure delivery; translating preparedness flyers advances equity.',
match('Health security priority and chapter activity',[['Community resilience','Neighborhood preparedness fair'],['Public health system','Helping at a flu vaccination clinic'],['Medical countermeasures','Volunteering at a POD exercise'],['Equity','Translating preparedness materials'],['Coordination','Joint planning meetings with the MRC'],['Workforce','Mentoring students toward health careers']])]],
['Disaster risk reduction',[
'**Disaster risk reduction (DRR)** aims to prevent new disaster risks, reduce existing risks and manage remaining risk. The United Nations **Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction (2015–2030)** sets global targets: reducing disaster deaths, the number of people affected, economic losses and damage to critical infrastructure such as hospitals and schools, and increasing early warning systems and disaster risk information.',
'A key DRR idea is that risk equals **hazard** combined with **exposure** and **vulnerability**. You cannot stop a hurricane (hazard), but you can reduce how many people are in harm’s way (exposure) and how badly they are affected (vulnerability) through planning, education and stronger systems.',
'DRR also emphasizes **local action** and the involvement of **all of society**, including young people. A youth-led activity that helps families make an emergency plan is a small but real example of disaster risk reduction.',
flip('Disaster risk reduction',[['Hazard','An event that can cause harm, like a flood'],['Exposure','People and property in harm’s way'],['Vulnerability','How susceptible people are to harm'],['Sendai Framework','UN plan for 2015–2030'],['Early warning','Alerts that give people time to act'],['Whole of society','Everyone, including youth, has a role']])]],
['Community resilience and vulnerable populations',[
'**Community resilience** is a community’s ability to **withstand, adapt to and recover** from adversity. Resilient communities have strong social connections, accessible health care, good communication, prepared individuals and organizations that work together. Neighbors checking on neighbors is one of the most powerful resilience factors.',
'Some people are at **higher risk** during disasters: older adults, young children, people with disabilities or chronic illnesses, people who depend on electricity for medical equipment, people with limited English, people experiencing homelessness and those without transportation. Planning that includes these groups from the start is called **whole community** planning.',
'One impact category for this event is **serving a vulnerable population**. Activities might include preparedness workshops at a senior center, translating materials, helping families who rely on medical devices make power-outage plans or supporting a clinic for people without regular health care.',
sort('Higher-risk group in disasters?',['Often higher risk','Generally lower risk'],[['An older adult who uses home oxygen',0],['A family with no car during an evacuation',0],['A person with limited English during an emergency alert',0],['A healthy adult with a car and supplies',1],['A child with severe asthma during wildfire smoke',0],['A person experiencing homelessness in a heat wave',0]])]],
['Youth and emergency planning',[
'FEMA and emergency managers recognize that **youth** can be powerful preparedness leaders. Young people bring preparedness lessons home, influence their families, use social media to share accurate information and can volunteer in age-appropriate roles. Including youth in planning also builds the next generation of emergency responders.',
'Basic preparedness messages for any audience include: **make a plan** (how to communicate, where to meet, how to evacuate), **build a kit** (water, food, medications, flashlight, batteries, first aid, documents, phone chargers—enough for at least **72 hours**), **stay informed** (sign up for local alerts) and **get involved** (learn first aid, join groups like the MRC or CERT).',
'Your chapter can teach these messages to classmates, families and community groups with your MRC partner’s guidance, tailoring them to your local hazards—such as hurricanes, earthquakes, wildfires, winter storms or heat.',
order('Personal preparedness steps',['Learn your local hazards','Make a family emergency plan','Build a kit for at least 72 hours','Sign up for local alerts','Learn first aid and CPR','Get involved with groups like the MRC'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing a focus',{a:{text:'Your MRC unit leader says many older adults in your county lost power for days during last winter’s storm and some needed emergency care. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which activity best fits?',choices:[['Power-outage preparedness workshops at senior centers, planned with the MRC','b','Correct. Serves a vulnerable population.',true],['A school dance','b','It does not address the need.',false]]},b:{text:'Which phase of emergency management does this workshop support?',choices:[['Preparedness','c','Correct.',true],['Recovery','c','It happens before the next emergency.',false]]},c:{text:'You plan to measure how many participants leave with a written power-outage plan.',end:true}})]],
["Emergencies MRC units respond to",["MRC units respond to many kinds of events. **Natural disasters** such as hurricanes, floods, tornadoes, wildfires and winter storms bring shelter operations and medical support. **Disease outbreaks**—influenza, measles, COVID-19 and others—bring vaccination clinics, testing sites and call centers. **Extreme heat and wildfire smoke** bring cooling centers and outreach to people at high risk.","Units also prepare for **mass casualty incidents**, **chemical or radiological events** and large **planned events** such as marathons and fairs. Knowing your community’s most likely hazards helps your chapter choose preparedness topics that matter locally.","Ask your MRC partner which recent events the unit responded to and what it learned. Those stories make powerful examples of the mission in your presentation.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Event and typical MRC role","pairs":[["Hurricane","Staffing a shelter medical station"],["Measles outbreak","Supporting vaccination clinics"],["Heat wave","Cooling centers and outreach"],["Marathon","First aid stations"],["Wildfire smoke","Distributing masks and guidance"],["Mass casualty drill","Triage and treatment practice"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Emergency management phases: mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery; MRC units contribute to every phase, and chapter activities can be mapped to them.','The National Health Security Strategy prioritizes community resilience, strong health systems, medical countermeasures, coordination and equity; the Sendai Framework (2015–2030) reduces disaster risk by lowering exposure and vulnerability.','Community resilience is the ability to withstand, adapt and recover; plan with vulnerable populations (older adults, people with disabilities or medical devices, limited English, no transportation); youth spread preparedness: make a plan, build a 72-hour kit, stay informed, get involved.'],terms:[['Community resilience','A community’s ability to withstand, adapt to and recover from adversity.'],['Mitigation','Actions that reduce the likelihood or impact of disasters.'],['Health security','Being prepared for, protected from and resilient to health threats.']],mcqs:[q('Which phase includes training and drills?','Preparedness','Recovery','Mitigation only','None','Before an emergency.'),q('Staffing a shelter during a hurricane is part of:','Response','Mitigation','Recovery','Prevention only','During the emergency.'),q('The Sendai Framework covers which years?','2015–2030','2000–2010','1990–2000','2030–2050','UN disaster risk reduction.'),q('In disaster risk reduction, risk combines hazard, exposure and:','Vulnerability','Popularity','Weather only','Cost','Susceptibility to harm.'),q('Which group is often at higher risk in disasters?','Older adults who depend on medical equipment','Healthy adults with supplies','None','Only professionals','Plan for them.'),q('A basic emergency kit should last at least:','72 hours','2 hours','1 month','1 year','Three days.'),q('Who prepares the National Health Security Strategy?','ASPR','WHO','FEMA only','The MRC unit','HHS office.'),q('Community resilience means the ability to:','Withstand, adapt to and recover from adversity','Avoid all disasters','Ignore risks','Rebuild only buildings','Social and system strength.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how community resilience, disaster risk reduction and the needs of vulnerable populations should guide the activities a HOSA chapter plans with its MRC unit.',rubric:[['Resilience',['withstand','adapt','recover','connections','neighbors','prepared']],['Risk reduction',['hazard','exposure','vulnerability','sendai','preparedness','mitigation']],['Vulnerable populations',['older adults','disabilities','medical equipment','limited english','transportation','whole community']],['Application',['activity','mrc','workshop','translate','plan','kit','measure','impact']]],model:'Community resilience is a community’s ability to withstand, adapt to and recover from emergencies, and it depends on prepared individuals, strong social connections, accessible health care and organizations that work together. Disaster risk reduction, described in the Sendai Framework for 2015–2030, explains that risk comes from a hazard combined with exposure and vulnerability; while we cannot stop a storm, we can reduce how many people are harmed and how badly. Because some people—older adults, people with disabilities or chronic illness, people who depend on electricity for medical devices, people with limited English and families without transportation—face greater risk, the National Health Security Strategy and whole-community planning stress including them from the start. These ideas should guide a chapter’s activities with its MRC unit. First, the chapter should ask the unit leader which local hazards and groups are of greatest concern. If older adults in the county struggled during power outages, the chapter could partner with the MRC to run preparedness workshops at senior centers, helping residents write power-outage plans, assemble 72-hour kits and sign up for alerts, with materials translated into the languages spoken locally. The chapter would measure impact by counting participants, hours contributed and the number of completed plans, and connect the activity to the MRC mission of building resilience and serving vulnerable populations.'},sources:[RULES,NHSS,SENDAI,READY_PLAN]};

export const MR3={title:'Finding your MRC unit and starting the partnership',hook:'Every successful partnership begins with a first conversation. For many chapters, that means a polite email to someone they have never met. Knowing how to find your local MRC unit, introduce your chapter professionally and agree on how you will work together sets the tone for the whole year.',objectives:['Locate a local MRC unit, state coordinator or regional liaison.','Write a professional first contact message.','Distinguish Path A (membership) from Path B (partnership).','Plan a productive first meeting with the unit leader.'],sections:[
['Finding your local unit',[
'To find a unit, visit the **MRC website** and use **“Join a Local MRC Unit”** or the **“Find a Local MRC Unit”** map, which lists units and contact information. If you cannot find or reach a unit, identify your **state coordinator** on the “About the MRC” page, or contact the **MRC regional liaison**, who can help connect you.',
'Look at more than one nearby unit if there are several. Some units are based in county health departments, others in cities, universities or hospitals. Read about each unit’s focus—some emphasize emergency response, others community health—and choose the one whose needs best match your chapter’s interests and location.',
'Start early in the year. Units are busy, and it may take several weeks to connect and complete the paperwork. Contact them as soon as possible after **July 1** so you have the full year for activities.',
order('Finding a partner',['Visit the MRC website','Use the “Find a Local MRC Unit” map','Read about nearby units','Contact the unit leader','If no response, contact the state coordinator','If still needed, ask the regional liaison'])]],
['Making professional first contact',[
'Your first message should be **short, polite and specific**. Include who you are (HOSA chapter, school, advisor), what HOSA is (a student organization for future health professionals), what you are asking for (a partnership for the MRC Partnership event between July 1 and May 15), what your chapter can offer (volunteers, outreach, youth perspective) and a request for a meeting. Attach or link the event guidelines.',
'Use a professional email address, a clear subject line (“HOSA chapter seeking MRC partnership”), correct grammar and a thank-you. Copy your **advisor**. Follow up politely after a week if you do not hear back. Remember that you are representing your school and HOSA in every message.',
'MRC unit leaders should **review the HOSA MRC Partnership guidelines** before agreeing, so sending them early helps the leader understand what the partnership involves.',
sort('Professional or unprofessional?',['Professional','Unprofessional'],[['Clear subject line and greeting',0],['“hey can u help us w/ a project”',1],['Explaining what HOSA is',0],['Copying your advisor',0],['Sending ten messages in one day',1],['Attaching the event guidelines',0]])]],
['Path A and Path B',[
'The guidelines describe two ways to engage. In **Path A: Membership**, students become **members (volunteers) of the local MRC unit**, completing any required registration, background checks or training the unit requires. In **Path B: Partnership**, the chapter **partners with the unit** without formal membership, working together on agreed activities.',
'Which path is possible depends on the unit’s **policies**—many units have **minimum ages** for membership, and some have youth programs or student roles. Discuss both options with the unit leader. Either path can lead to strong activities; what matters is that the partnership is real, active and outside the classroom.',
'The **Partnership Logistics Document** asks you to identify whether the HOSA chapter will be **considered volunteers** with the MRC unit, so decide this together at the first meeting.',
match('Path and description',[['Path A: Membership','Students join the MRC unit as volunteers'],['Path B: Partnership','Chapter partners without formal membership'],['Age requirements','Set by each unit’s policies'],['Background checks or training','May be required for membership'],['Logistics Document','States whether students are volunteers'],['Either path','Must be an active partnership outside the classroom']])]],
['The first meeting',[
'Prepare an **agenda**: introductions; an overview of HOSA and the event; the unit’s mission, priorities and upcoming events; possible activities; Path A or B; communication plans; required forms; and next steps. Bring the guidelines and forms. Dress professionally and arrive on time, whether the meeting is in person or online.',
'Ask good questions: What community needs is the unit focused on this year? What upcoming events need volunteers? What training would students need? What roles are appropriate for students? How should we report our activities? Who will be our main contact? Take notes and summarize decisions in a follow-up email.',
'The guidelines ask MRC unit leaders to submit the **first meeting** between HOSA and the MRC in the **MRC Activity Reporting System**, so share the meeting date, participants and length to make this easy.',
order('First meeting agenda',['Introductions','Overview of HOSA and the event','MRC priorities and upcoming events','Brainstorm activities','Choose Path A or Path B','Agree on communication and forms','Confirm next steps'])]],
['Building a strong working relationship',[
'Strong partnerships rely on **clear communication**, **reliability** and **mutual respect**. Respond to messages promptly, show up when you say you will and follow the unit’s procedures and safety rules. Assign one or two **team contacts** so the unit leader is not overwhelmed by messages from many students.',
'Remember that the unit’s needs come first. Ask how your chapter can help rather than only what the MRC can do for your portfolio. Offer to help with small tasks—creating a flyer, translating information, setting up for a clinic. Thank volunteers and leaders publicly (with approval) and privately.',
'Keep a **contact log** of every meeting, call and email, including dates and topics. It helps you write the partnership description and demonstrates the level, quality and quantity of your interactions.',
chk('Who should the MRC unit leader mainly communicate with?',['One or two designated team contacts','Every team member separately','Only the school principal','No one'],'Clear, consistent communication keeps the partnership manageable.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('No reply',{a:{text:'You emailed the nearest MRC unit two weeks ago and received no reply. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Send a polite follow-up and, if needed, contact the state coordinator or regional liaison','b','Correct.',true],['Give up on the event','b','Use the other contacts the guidelines list.',false]]},b:{text:'The unit leader replies and says students under 18 cannot be formal members. What now?',choices:[['Discuss Path B: a partnership on agreed activities','c','Correct.',true],['Pretend to be 18','c','Always follow unit policies honestly.',false]]},c:{text:'You schedule a first meeting with an agenda and copy your advisor.',end:true}})]],
["A model first-contact email",["A clear, professional email makes a strong first impression. Keep it under about 200 words, use a specific subject line, greet the unit leader by name and title and sign with your full name, chapter, school and advisor’s contact information.",{"t":"example","title":"Writing the email","prompt":"Write a first-contact email to an invented MRC unit leader.","steps":["Subject: HOSA chapter requesting an MRC partnership for 2026–27","Greeting: “Dear Ms. Rivera,”","Who we are: HOSA chapter at an invented high school; advisor copied","What HOSA is: a student organization for future health professionals","The request: partner on activities between July 1 and May 15 for the MRC Partnership event","What we offer: volunteers, outreach to families, help with materials","Next step: “Could we meet for 30 minutes in the next two weeks?”","Close: thank-you, full name, chapter and advisor contact; guidelines attached"],"answer":"A short, specific, polite message that makes it easy for the leader to say yes (example invented for practice)."},"Read the email aloud before sending, check names and spelling and have your advisor review it. A careless first message can make it harder to build trust."]]
],takeaways:['Find a unit with the MRC website’s “Find a Local MRC Unit” map; if needed, contact the state coordinator or regional liaison; start soon after July 1.','First contact should be short and professional—who you are, what HOSA is, what you ask and offer, the guidelines attached, the advisor copied—with polite follow-up.','Path A is membership as MRC volunteers; Path B is partnership without membership; unit policies such as age decide; plan a first meeting with an agenda, ask about needs and roles, name contacts, keep a contact log and help the unit report the first meeting.'],terms:[['Path A','Students become members of the local MRC unit.'],['Path B','The chapter partners with the MRC unit without formal membership.'],['Regional liaison','An MRC staff member who helps connect chapters and units.']],mcqs:[q('Where do you find a local MRC unit?','The MRC website’s “Find a Local MRC Unit” map','The phone book only','A social media influencer','HOSA’s national office only','Then contact the unit leader.'),q('If you cannot reach a unit, whom can you contact?','The state coordinator or regional liaison','No one','The judges','A celebrity','Listed in the guidelines.'),q('What is Path A?','Membership in the local MRC unit','A partnership without membership','A school club only','A social media campaign','Volunteers.'),q('What is Path B?','Partnership with the MRC unit','Membership in the unit','Working alone','Online research only','Without formal membership.'),q('What should a first contact email include?','Who you are, what HOSA is and what you are asking for','Only “Help us”','Personal opinions','Nothing','Short and specific.'),q('Who should be copied on emails to the MRC?','Your HOSA advisor','Every student in school','The judges','No one','Professionalism.'),q('Which document states whether students are MRC volunteers?','Partnership Logistics Document','Title page','Reference page','Resume','Agreed terms.'),q('Why keep a contact log?','To document the level, quality and quantity of interactions','To count likes','It is not useful','To skip meetings','Supports the portfolio.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your chapter would find a local MRC unit, make first contact and plan a first meeting that sets up a strong partnership.',rubric:[['Finding',['mrc website','find a local','map','unit leader','state coordinator','regional liaison']],['First contact',['professional','hosa','event','guidelines','offer','advisor','follow up']],['Path and meeting',['path a','path b','membership','partnership','agenda','questions','needs','roles']],['Relationship',['communication','contact','log','reliable','reporting system','first meeting']]],model:'Soon after July 1, our team would use the “Find a Local MRC Unit” map on the MRC website to identify nearby units and read about their focus areas. We would email the unit leader of the best match with a clear subject line, introducing our chapter, school and advisor, explaining that HOSA is an organization for future health professionals and asking to partner for the MRC Partnership event between July 1 and May 15. We would describe what we can offer—volunteers, outreach to students and families and help with materials—attach the event guidelines so the leader can review them and copy our advisor. If we did not hear back within a week, we would follow up politely, and if needed contact the state coordinator or regional liaison. For the first meeting, we would prepare an agenda covering introductions, HOSA and the event, the unit’s priorities and upcoming events, activity ideas, whether we will follow Path A by becoming members or Path B by partnering without membership based on the unit’s age policies, communication plans and the required forms. We would ask what community needs the unit is focused on and which roles are appropriate for students. Afterward we would send a summary email, name two team contacts, start a contact log and give the unit leader the meeting details to enter in the MRC Activity Reporting System.'},sources:[RULES,MRC,MRC_ABOUT]};

export const MR4={title:'The partnership forms and reporting',hook:'Paperwork may not be exciting, but in MRC Partnership it is worth points—and missing it can cost you the event. Several forms document that your partnership is real, agreed upon and accurately reported. Knowing what each form needs and when it must be signed keeps your team on track all year.',objectives:['Describe the eight steps of the competitive event process.','Complete the Partnership Notification Form, Verification Form and Logistics Document.','Explain when the MRC unit leader signs each form.','Support the MRC unit leader in reporting activities.'],sections:[
['The eight-step process',[
'The guidelines list eight steps: **(1)** review the recommended readings; **(2)** discuss engagement with the local MRC unit (Path A membership or Path B partnership); **(3)** complete the **Office of the MRC’s Partnership Notification Form**; **(4)** complete the **Partnership Verification Form** and **Partnership Logistics Document**; **(5)** begin partnership activities and demonstrate impact, taking photographs at events; **(6)** track activities and prepare descriptions for the portfolio; **(7)** at the end, have the **MRC unit leader review the portfolio and sign the Verification Form again**; and **(8)** have one team member upload the portfolio.',
'Treat these steps as a **timeline**. Steps 1–4 should be done early in the fall so activities can begin. Steps 5–6 continue throughout the year. Steps 7–8 must be completed before each conference deadline—regional, state and international, as applicable.',
'Create a shared team calendar with these milestones and assign an owner for each form so nothing is forgotten.',
order('Competitive event process',['Review the recommended readings','Discuss Path A or Path B with the MRC unit','Complete the Partnership Notification Form','Complete the Verification Form and Logistics Document','Begin activities and take photographs','Track activities and write descriptions','MRC leader reviews the portfolio and signs again','One member uploads the portfolio'])]],
['The Partnership Verification Form',[
'The **HOSA/MRC Partnership Verification Form** outlines the partnership agreement. It lists the **HOSA chapter**, **team member names**, **school address**, **advisor name and email**, **MRC unit name and address**, and the **unit leader’s name, email, phone number and mailing address**. The competitor and the MRC unit leader sign to verify they have read the guidelines and agree to the terms in the Logistics Document.',
'The form also includes **HOSA/MRC Impact Data**—the **total hours volunteered with the MRC** by all team members across the project year. At the end, the unit leader signs again to verify that the portfolio is an **accurate representation** of the partnership, **before each applicable regional, state and international conference**.',
'On the rating sheet, the Verification Form earns points only if it includes all four items: the **MRC unit’s full address**, the **leader’s signature at the beginning**, the **leader’s signature at the conclusion** and the **impact data summary numbers**.',
flip('Verification Form must include',[['MRC unit address','Full address'],['Beginning signature','MRC leader signs at the start'],['Ending signature','MRC leader signs after reviewing the portfolio'],['Impact data','Total hours volunteered with the MRC'],['Contacts','Advisor and unit leader information'],['When re-signed','Before each applicable conference']])]],
['The Partnership Logistics Document',[
'The **Partnership Logistics Document** (no more than **two pages**) records the **agreed-upon terms** of the partnership. It answers: **(1)** how the HOSA chapter and MRC unit will maintain contact during the year; **(2)** how frequently they will contact each other; **(3)** who is responsible for initiating and maintaining contact; and **(4)** how HOSA will support the MRC unit leader in submitting completed activities in the **MRC Activity Reporting System** (information needed, preferred format, frequency).',
'It should also identify whether the HOSA chapter will be **considered volunteers** with the MRC unit and include any specific requirements for the partnership. The MRC leader signs it at the **beginning** of the partnership and **again** after reviewing the finalized portfolio, and the leader’s **full mailing address** is required.',
'The rating sheet awards full points only when the document includes the communication method, frequency, who is responsible, the leader’s mailing address and how the chapter supported reporting. Answer every question completely.',
match('Logistics question and example answer',[['How will we stay in contact?','Monthly video call and email updates'],['How often?','At least once a month, plus before each event'],['Who initiates contact?','The team’s two designated contacts'],['How will HOSA support reporting?','Send an activity summary within a week of each event'],['Are students volunteers?','Path B: partners, not members'],['Required detail','MRC leader’s full mailing address']])]],
['The MRC Activity Reporting System',[
'MRC units record their activities in the **MRC Activity Reporting System**, which helps the national program document volunteer contributions and community impact. The unit leader should report the **first meeting** between HOSA and the MRC and then the partnership activities, either **as they occur** or **consolidated** with similar activities.',
'Your chapter supports this by sending the unit leader a short **activity summary** after each event: date, location, activity name and description, impact category, number of students and hours, number of people served or trained, and any photos approved for use. Agree on the format and timing in your Logistics Document.',
'Good reporting benefits the unit, too—it shows the value of its volunteers and partners. Make it easy for the leader by being consistent and on time.',
order('After-event activity summary',['Date and location','Activity name and description','Impact category','Number of students and total hours','Number of people served or trained','Approved photos','Send to the unit leader within the agreed time'])]],
['Social media and publicity approvals',[
'**All social media posts** for the MRC Partnership event **must be approved by the MRC partner** before posting. Failure to do so may result in **losing the opportunity to compete**. Build approval into your workflow: draft the post, send it to the unit leader, wait for written approval and then publish. Keep the approval emails.',
'The same caution applies to **press releases**, **flyers** and **interviews**. Use the MRC’s correct name and logo only with permission, follow your school’s media policy and get consent from anyone pictured. Publicity can be included in the portfolio—with the publicity date and a copy of the article, radio or TV spot or script.',
'Approvals protect everyone: the MRC unit’s reputation, the privacy of community members served and your team’s eligibility.',
chk('What must happen before a team posts about an MRC activity on social media?',['The MRC partner approves the post','Nothing','The post gets 100 likes','A judge approves it'],'Unapproved posts may cost the team the event.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Form check',{a:{text:'In April, your team reviews the Verification Form. It has the unit leader’s beginning signature and total hours but not the unit’s full address. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the problem?',choices:[['All four required items are needed for the points—add the full address','b','Correct.',true],['It is fine as is','b','Missing items earn zero for that criterion.',false]]},b:{text:'What else must happen before the state conference deadline?',choices:[['The unit leader reviews the finished portfolio and signs again','c','Correct.',true],['Nothing—signatures from fall are enough','c','A conclusion signature is required.',false]]},c:{text:'You send the leader the portfolio with time to review, receive the signed form and upload one PDF before the deadline.',end:true}})]],
["Keeping records organized",["With many forms, approvals, photos and hours, organization prevents last-minute panic. Create a **shared team folder** with subfolders for **forms**, **approvals**, **activities** (one folder each), **photos** and **portfolio drafts**. Use clear file names with dates, such as “2026-10-12_FluClinic_SignIn.pdf.”","Keep a **master tracker** listing each activity, date, impact category, students, hours, people impacted, MRC approval status and whether the activity summary was sent to the unit leader. Update it after every event.","Save signed forms as PDFs and keep the original email approvals. When it is time to build the portfolio, everything will be in one place.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Record-keeping routine after each activity","steps":["Save sign-in sheets and calculate hours","Upload dated photos with captions","File the MRC approval emails","Update the master tracker","Send the activity summary to the unit leader","Note lessons learned"]}]]
],takeaways:['Eight steps: readings, Path A or B, Partnership Notification Form, Verification Form and Logistics Document, activities with photos, tracking and descriptions, MRC leader review and second signature, upload by one member.','Verification Form needs the MRC unit’s full address, the leader’s beginning and concluding signatures and impact data (total hours volunteered); the leader re-signs before each applicable conference.','Logistics Document (≤2 pages) answers how, how often and who maintains contact and how HOSA supports reporting in the MRC Activity Reporting System, states whether students are volunteers and includes the leader’s mailing address; all social media posts need MRC approval.'],terms:[['Partnership Verification Form','The signed agreement and impact summary for the HOSA–MRC partnership.'],['Partnership Logistics Document','A two-page statement of how the partnership will communicate and report.'],['MRC Activity Reporting System','The system MRC units use to record activities and volunteer contributions.']],mcqs:[q('How long may the Partnership Logistics Document be?','No more than two pages','Ten pages','One paragraph','Unlimited','Agreed terms.'),q('Which item must appear on the Verification Form?','Total hours volunteered with the MRC','Each student’s GPA','The school mascot','Team T-shirt sizes','Impact data.'),q('When does the MRC leader sign the Verification Form again?','After reviewing the finished portfolio, before each applicable conference','Never','Only in July','After ILC','Accuracy check.'),q('Who reports activities in the MRC Activity Reporting System?','The MRC unit leader, with HOSA’s support','The judges','HOSA national','No one','Chapter sends summaries.'),q('What must be approved by the MRC partner before posting?','All social media posts about the event','Only videos','Nothing','Only photos of the leader','Or risk losing eligibility.'),q('Which is step 3 of the process?','Complete the Partnership Notification Form','Upload the portfolio','Present to judges','Take photographs','Office of the MRC form.'),q('The Logistics Document must include the MRC leader’s:','Full mailing address','Favorite color','Birthday','Social media handle','Required detail.'),q('Who uploads the portfolio?','One team member','Every team member','The MRC leader','The judges','One PDF.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the forms required for MRC Partnership, what each must contain and how your team would make sure they are completed and signed on time.',rubric:[['Process',['readings','path','notification form','verification form','logistics','photographs','track','sign again','upload']],['Verification Form',['address','beginning','conclusion','signature','impact data','hours']],['Logistics Document',['two pages','contact','frequency','responsible','reporting system','volunteers','mailing address']],['Timeline and approvals',['calendar','owner','before each conference','social media','approved','summary']]],model:'Early in the fall, after reviewing the recommended readings and agreeing with our MRC unit on Path A or Path B, we would complete the Office of the MRC’s Partnership Notification Form, then the HOSA/MRC Partnership Verification Form and the Partnership Logistics Document. The Verification Form lists our chapter, team members, school address and advisor, the MRC unit’s full address and the unit leader’s contact and mailing information; both a competitor and the unit leader sign it at the start, and it records our impact data—the total hours all team members volunteered with the MRC. The Logistics Document, no more than two pages, explains how we will stay in contact, such as a monthly video call and email updates, how often we will be in touch, who is responsible for initiating contact—our two team contacts—and how we will support the leader in entering activities in the MRC Activity Reporting System by sending a summary with date, description, impact category, hours and people served within a week of each event. It also states whether we are considered MRC volunteers and includes the leader’s full mailing address, and the leader signs it at the beginning and again at the end. To stay on time, we would put each step on a shared calendar with an owner, get MRC approval for every social media post, send the finished portfolio to the leader at least two weeks before each conference deadline for review and a second signature and then have one team member upload the single PDF.'},sources:[RULES,MRC_ABOUT,MRC]};

export const MR5={title:'Choosing activities: the seven impact categories',hook:'Distributing emergency kit lists at a store, mentoring with MRC nurses and playing moulaged victims in a mass casualty drill are all real examples from the event guidelines. Each fits an impact category that tells judges what difference the activity made. Choosing the right mix of meaningful activities is the core of a strong portfolio.',objectives:['Describe the seven impact categories and give examples of each.','Match activities to community needs identified with the MRC.','Apply “quality over quantity” when planning activities.','Choose safe, age-appropriate roles for students.'],sections:[
['The seven impact categories',[
'Every activity in your portfolio is labeled with **one impact category**: **(1) strengthen public health**; **(2) serve a vulnerable population**; **(3) support a non-emergency community event**; **(4) develop or strengthen the HOSA/MRC partnership**; **(5) improve community preparedness or resilience**; **(6) train or exercise to improve community response capability**; and **(7) support an emergency response**.',
'Choose the category that best describes the activity’s **main** impact. A flu clinic strengthens public health; a preparedness talk at a senior center might be “serve a vulnerable population” or “improve community preparedness”—pick the one your description emphasizes and explain why.',
'A portfolio that covers **several categories** shows the breadth of your partnership, but each activity must still be meaningful. Do not add weak activities just to fill a category.',
match('Activity and impact category',[['Helping at a flu vaccination clinic','Strengthen public health'],['Preparedness workshop at a senior center','Serve a vulnerable population'],['First aid tent at a community 5K','Support a non-emergency community event'],['Shadowing MRC volunteers','Develop or strengthen the HOSA/MRC partnership'],['Distributing 72-hour kit lists','Improve community preparedness or resilience'],['Role-playing victims in a disaster drill','Train or exercise to improve response capability']])]],
['Examples from the guidelines',[
'**Sample 1:** students distributed **72-hour emergency kit supply lists** at a local store during back-to-school shopping. **Impact:** improved community preparedness or resilience. The team met with the MRC unit leader, who advised on kit contents for the community’s hazards; the team spent **20 hours** and reached **100 people**.',
'**Sample 2:** a **shadowing and mentoring program** paired students with MRC volunteers in their area of interest. **Impact:** developed or strengthened the HOSA/MRC partnership. Five members participated for 30 hours each—**150 hours**. **Sample 3:** students were **moulaged** (made up with realistic simulated injuries) as patients in a **school bus crash drill**. **Impact:** training or exercise to improve response capability. Twenty-five members participated, supporting training for **40 people**, for **100 total hours**.',
'Notice that each example includes the **activity**, the **impact category** and a **partnership interaction description** with the MRC’s role and **key metrics** (hours and people impacted). Your portfolio entries should follow the same pattern.',
{t:'table',caption:'Guideline sample activities',head:['Activity','Impact category','Key metrics'],rows:[['72-hour kit supply lists at a store','Improve community preparedness or resilience','20 hours; 100 people reached'],['Shadowing and mentoring program','Develop or strengthen the HOSA/MRC partnership','5 members × 30 hours = 150 hours'],['Moulaged victims in a bus crash drill','Train or exercise to improve response capability','25 members; 40 trained; 100 hours']]}]],
['Starting from community needs',[
'The strongest activities answer a **specific community need** identified with your MRC partner. Ask the unit leader about recent emergencies, health department priorities, upcoming events, groups that are hard to reach and gaps in volunteer coverage. Look at local data on hazards, vaccination rates or chronic disease, and listen to community members.',
'Then design activities where your chapter can make a **real difference**. If vaccination rates are low among children, you might help with school-based clinics and create family-friendly reminders. If heat waves are rising, you might help staff cooling centers or distribute heat-safety information to older adults and outdoor workers.',
'Judges score **cooperative work with the MRC to reach goals** and **impact on the local community**. A clear need, a planned response and measured results tell a strong story.',
order('From need to activity',['Ask the MRC about local needs','Review local data and hazards','Choose a specific need','Design an activity with the MRC','Set goals and metrics','Carry it out and measure impact'])]],
['Quality over quantity',[
'The guidelines remind teams that **quality over quantity** of activities is important. A few well-planned activities with clear goals, strong MRC collaboration and measurable impact score better than many brief appearances. Judges rate the **quality, scope and value** of activities.',
'Signs of a **high-quality** activity include: it meets a need the MRC identified; students had meaningful roles; it was planned with the MRC; it reached many people or a high-need group; it was measured; and it led to follow-up or a lasting change. **Lower-quality** activities include one-time attendance without a role or activities unrelated to the MRC mission.',
'**Imagination and creativity** are also scored in Round Two. Look for fresh approaches—a bilingual preparedness video approved by the MRC, a “Stop the Bleed” training for coaches or a preparedness escape room for middle schoolers.',
sort('Higher or lower quality activity?',['Higher quality','Lower quality'],[['Planning and running a preparedness fair with the MRC for 200 families',0],['Attending one MRC meeting without a role',1],['Creating MRC-approved bilingual heat-safety flyers for outdoor workers',0],['A bake sale unrelated to the MRC mission',1],['Serving as moulaged victims in a county disaster drill',0]])]],
['Safe and appropriate student roles',[
'Students must work within their **training and age**. Appropriate roles include **registration and check-in**, **greeting and wayfinding**, **distributing information**, **translation** (when qualified), **setting up and cleaning up**, **playing victims in drills**, **creating approved educational materials**, **assisting with trainings** and **supporting logistics**. Clinical tasks—giving vaccines or medications—are reserved for licensed professionals.',
'Follow the unit’s **safety rules**: wear identification, use PPE when required, stay with your assigned supervisor, take breaks and hydrate, and never self-deploy to an emergency scene. During real emergencies, students should help **only** as directed by the MRC and with parent and school permission.',
'Keep your advisor informed and follow school policies for transportation, permission slips and supervision.',
sort('Appropriate student role?',['Appropriate','Not appropriate'],[['Checking in clients at a vaccination clinic',0],['Giving vaccine injections without a license',1],['Playing a moulaged victim in a drill',0],['Self-deploying to a disaster scene',1],['Handing out preparedness flyers',0],['Translating approved materials',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Planning the year',{a:{text:'Your MRC leader says the county wants more families prepared for wildfire smoke, and the unit is running a regional evacuation drill in March. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which plan is strongest?',choices:[['A smoke-preparedness campaign for families plus playing victims in the March drill, both planned with the MRC','b','Correct. Two meaningful activities tied to needs.',true],['Ten unrelated activities to fill every category','b','Quality over quantity.',false]]},b:{text:'Which impact category fits the drill?',choices:[['Train or exercise to improve community response capability','c','Correct.',true],['Support a non-emergency community event','c','A drill is a training exercise.',false]]},c:{text:'You set metrics for both: families reached, kits or plans completed, students and hours, and responders trained.',end:true}})]],
["Ideas across the categories",["**Strengthen public health:** help with registration at vaccination clinics, assist at health screenings or create MRC-approved materials about handwashing or heat safety. **Serve a vulnerable population:** preparedness workshops at senior centers, translated materials for immigrant families or kits for people experiencing homelessness.","**Support a non-emergency community event:** staff information or first aid support tables at fairs, races or festivals under MRC supervision. **Develop or strengthen the partnership:** joint trainings, mentoring and shadowing, or recruiting new MRC volunteers. **Improve preparedness or resilience:** preparedness fairs, kit-building events and family plan workshops.","**Train or exercise:** serve as moulaged victims, help with drill logistics or co-host CPR and Stop the Bleed classes. **Support an emergency response:** only when the MRC requests help, with permissions and supervision, in roles such as registration or supply sorting.",{"t":"check","q":"When may students support an actual emergency response?","options":["Only when the MRC requests help, with permissions and supervision","Whenever they hear about a disaster","Never under any circumstances","Only on weekends"],"why":"Never self-deploy."}]]
],takeaways:['Seven impact categories: strengthen public health; serve a vulnerable population; support a non-emergency community event; develop or strengthen the HOSA/MRC partnership; improve community preparedness or resilience; train or exercise to improve response capability; support an emergency response.','Each activity entry needs a name and description, one impact category and a partnership interaction description with the MRC’s role and key metrics (hours and people impacted), as in the guideline samples.','Start from a specific community need identified with the MRC; quality, scope, value and creativity beat quantity; students take safe, nonclinical, supervised roles and never self-deploy.'],terms:[['Impact category','The type of community impact an activity provides.'],['Moulage','Realistic simulated injuries applied for training exercises.'],['Key metrics','Numbers such as hours contributed and people impacted.']],mcqs:[q('How many impact categories are listed in the guidelines?','Seven','Three','Ten','Two','One per activity.'),q('Distributing 72-hour kit supply lists fits which category?','Improve community preparedness or resilience','Support an emergency response','Serve a vulnerable population only','None','Guideline sample 1.'),q('Playing moulaged victims in a drill fits which category?','Train or exercise to improve community response capability','Strengthen public health','Support a non-emergency event','Develop the partnership','Guideline sample 3.'),q('A shadowing and mentoring program fits which category?','Develop or strengthen the HOSA/MRC partnership','Support an emergency response','Serve a vulnerable population','None','Guideline sample 2.'),q('Which three items does each activity entry include?','Name and description, impact category and partnership interaction description','Only photos','Only hours','A song, poem and map','Required items.'),q('Which is an appropriate student role?','Checking in clients at a clinic','Giving injections without a license','Self-deploying to disasters','Prescribing medication','Nonclinical.'),q('What does “quality over quantity” mean?','Fewer meaningful activities with impact beat many small ones','Do as many activities as possible','Only one activity is allowed','Activities do not matter','Judges rate quality and value.'),q('A first aid tent at a community 5K fits which category?','Support a non-emergency community event','Support an emergency response','Train or exercise','Serve a vulnerable population','Non-emergency event.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe two partnership activities your chapter could plan with its MRC unit, including the community need, impact category, student roles and key metrics for each.',rubric:[['Need',['need','mrc','identified','data','hazard','community']],['Activities and categories',['activity','impact category','preparedness','public health','vulnerable','train','exercise','event']],['Roles and partnership',['role','mrc','planned','supervisor','nonclinical','safe','interaction']],['Metrics',['hours','people','trained','reached','measured','quality']]],model:'Our MRC unit leader told us the county’s biggest concerns this year are low preparedness for extended power outages among older adults and the need to train responders for a mass casualty incident. Our first activity is a series of power-outage preparedness workshops at three senior centers, labeled “serve a vulnerable population.” The MRC leader will review our materials and send a nurse volunteer to answer medical-device questions, while students greet participants, help them complete a written plan and kit checklist and translate materials into Spanish. We will measure the number of students and total hours, the number of seniors attending and the number who leave with a completed plan—our goal is 75. Our second activity is serving as moulaged victims in the county’s spring mass casualty drill, labeled “train or exercise to improve community response capability.” The MRC leader invited us, arranged moulage training with the exercise team and assigned each student a patient card; during the drill, students follow the script while MRC and EMS volunteers practice triage and treatment. We will track the number of students, hours contributed and responders trained. Both activities meet needs the MRC identified, give students safe, nonclinical roles under supervision and produce clear metrics, showing quality rather than quantity.'},sources:[RULES,MRC,READY_KIT]};
